TABLE: INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF SCIENCE FOR GLOBAL SUSTAINABILITY | (A PUBLICATION OF FACULTY OF SCIENCE, FEDERAL UNIVERSITY GUSAU, NIGERIA) | Soil Contamination and Food Chain Transfer in Niger State, Nigeria | Mohammed Abubakar Mohammed1, Angwa Egbe Monday2, Mary Aderonke Oguntuase3, Abubakar | Department of Geology, Federal University of Lafia, Nigeria | Jacmed Geo Centre, Nasarawa State University, Keffi, Nigeria | Department of Geology, Ekiti State University, Ado Ekiti, Nigeria | Department of Geological Technology, Isa Mustapha Agwai Polytechnic Lafia, Nigeria | The issue of soil pollution has taken on a rather acute environmental and human health situation in agrarian | Nigerian states, especially in states like Niger where agricultural activities constitute the main source of living in | the rural areas. The penetration of heavy metals, agrochemical residues, hydrocarbons and other enduring | pollutants in the agricultural soils are very dangerous because it will be transferred to food crops and will finally | processes in some of the selected agricultural and mining affected communities in Niger state. Priority is given to | such contaminants as: lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), arsenic (As) and mercury (Hg), chromium (Cr), and zinc (Zn) | which are often related to artisanal mining, excessive use of fertilizers, industrial effluents, and random dumping | of waste. Based on modern environmental-geochemical methods, the study combines the physicochemical | analysis of the soil, crop-uptake analysis, the transfer-factor analysis, exposure-risk analysis to evaluate the | magnitudes and the trophic mobility of contamination. The representative samples of soil and crops are analyzed | to identify the concentration of heavy-metals and their distribution of accumulation in soil and plants. Geospatial | methods are used to map the hot spots of contamination, test the spatial variation and find the communities that | are at a significant risk of exposure. The paper also uses human-health risk assessment indices to describe the | possible non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic risks that are related to the consumption of contaminated food crops. | Recent empirical research in Niger State shows that the soils located near the mining areas and agricultural settings | that have high agrochemical application have high levels of heavy-metals. A number of food crops are also highly | bio accumulative; especially the leafy vegetables and root tubers; their transfer-factors are higher than the advised

