The target hazard quotient (THQ) is calculated to
estimate the potential risk of these heavy metals
(USEPA, 2000). The equation is:
(2)

Here, EDI represents the estimated daily intake of
metals (mg/day), RfDs indicates reference dose; the
reference doses for Fe, Cu, Mn, Zn, Pb, Cd, Cr and As
are 0.7, 0.04, 0.05, 0.3, 0.004,0.001, 1.5 and 0.0003 mg/
kg BW-1 day-1 9 (USEPA , 2010). If the value of THQs is
equal to or higher than 1, indicates the potential health
risk for this heavy metal (Islam et al., 2011). When
consumers are exposed to two or more pollutants, they
may suffer from adverse or interactive effects
(Hallenbeck, 1993). The total THQ (TTHQ) of these
heavy metals is the mathematical sum of the THQ values
of each individual metal. TTHQ values can be estimated
by the following equation:
TTHQ = THQ toxic element 1 + THQ toxic element 2 (3)
+ ... + THQ toxic element n
2.4.3 Target carcinogenic risk (TCR)
When the carcinogens are to be exposed over a
lifetime, the probability of cancer of an individual can be
developed (USEPA, 1989). The acceptable risk level
