Abstract
Carrots and cucumbers are highly consumed root and fruit vegetables that
constitute a significant portion of the diet. However, pollutants such as heavy
metals in these vegetables can lead to health consequences for consumers. In this
study, heavy metal concentrations in carrots and cucumbers in southern Iran were
analyzed, and the carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic risks from their consumption
were assessed using Monte Carlo simulation. The results showed that the average
concentrations of chromium, cadmium, nickel, and lead in cucumber samples
(0.175, 0.003, 0.136, and 0.075 mg kg− 1) were lower than those in carrots (0.654,
0.004, 0.409, and 0.303 mg kg− 1). Hazard Index (HI) due to the concentrations of the
studied heavy metals in cucumber and carrots were 2.31E-3 and 8.2E-3 respectively.
The order of metals effective in Hazard Quotient (HQ) in studied vegetables were
Pb > Ni > Cd > Cr. The Estimated Daily Intake (EDI) of the total studied metals resulting
from the consumption of cucumber and carrot was 1.1 and 2.2, respectively.
Therefore, the calculated carcinogenic risk based on the Excess Lifetime Cancer
Risk (ELCR) associated with cucumber and carrot consumption was 2.32E-5 and
1.00E-4, respectively. Based on results, the concentrations of studied heavy metals
in cucumbers and carrots may lead to potential long-term health concerns if not
controlled in a plan to identify sources of emissions and reduce these pollutants in
agricultural land and water.
Keywords Risk assessment, Heavy metals, Monte carlo simulation, Food safety, Trace
elements
