+
+# Mohiuddin et al. 2015 - Heavy metals in edible mushrooms from Bangladesh
+
+This Bangladesh study measured protein, K, Na, Fe, Cu, Zn, Mn, Cr, Pb, As, and Cd in edible mushroom varieties, including Pleurotus ostreatus, Agaricus bisporus, Volvariella volvacea, and Ganoderma lucidum. The source reports dry-weight concentrations and states that Pb ranged from 0.13 to 0.59 ug/g, while As and Cd were below the flame-AAS detection limit of 0.01 ug/g. It is routeable to cultivated mushroom evidence, with total arsenic below detection and no arsenic speciation.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+All element concentrations are reported on a dry-weight basis.
+
+- Species scope: Pleurotus ostreatus, Agaricus bisporus, Volvariella volvacea, Ganoderma lucidum.
+- Protein: 13.8%-34.3%.
+- K: 0.54%-2.25%.
+- Na: 12.6-81.6 ug/g.
+- Fe: 69.5-626.2 ug/g; mean 188.2 ug/g.
+- Cu: 39.2-163.4 ug/g; mean 85.88 ug/g.
+- Zn: 30.1-75.5 ug/g; mean 43.24 ug/g.
+- Mn: 52.9-104.5 ug/g; mean 75.70 ug/g.
+- Cr: 0.20-0.30 ug/g; mean 0.25 ug/g.
+- Pb: 0.13-0.59 ug/g; mean 0.19 ug/g.
+- As and Cd: below detectable limit; method detection limit 0.01 ug/g for each.
+
+The authors state that all Pb results were below the WHO permissible limit cited for plants (2.0 ug/g). They also report that Cu and Zn in some mushroom samples were higher than legal limits.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+Mushroom samples were washed with deionized water, dried at 105 C for 24 hours, and analyzed on a dry-weight basis. Trace metals were measured by atomic absorption spectrophotometer. Sodium and potassium were measured by flame emission spectrophotometer, and protein by the micro-Kjeldahl method. Arsenic is reported as total As below detection; no arsenic speciation was performed.
+
+## Implications
+
+Certification: This paper contributes Bangladesh cultivated-mushroom values for Pb, Cr, and supporting mineral context. It is not a Cd occurrence source beyond a non-detect at 0.01 ug/g.
+
+Courses: Useful example of how papers can report both nutritionally essential metals and toxic metals in the same mushroom table; Pb and Cd require separate interpretation from Fe, Cu, Zn, and Mn.
+
+App: Supports a low-Pb cultivated mushroom data point for the Bangladesh samples, but does not remove the need for wild-mushroom higher-risk flags from other sources.
+
+## Wiki pages this source may touch
+
