+# Siric et al. 2019 - Heavy metals in cultivated mushroom species in Croatia
+
+This Croatian study measured heavy metals in three cultivated mushroom species, with 20 samples per species. ICP-OES was used for most metals and direct-combustion AMA-254 for mercury. The highest average Pb and total As were found in Pleurotus ostreatus, while the highest average Cd was found in Lentinula edodes.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+All values are reported in the abstract; Fe, Zn, and Cu are mg/kg, while Hg, Pb, As, and Cd are reported as ug/kg.
+
+- Sample size: 20 samples each of Agaricus bisporus, Pleurotus ostreatus, and Lentinula edodes (n=60 total).
+- Highest average Fe: 60.04 mg/kg in Agaricus bisporus.
+- Highest average Zn: 53.64 mg/kg in Agaricus bisporus.
+- Highest average Cu: 7.06 mg/kg in Agaricus bisporus.
+- Highest average Hg: 2.27 ug/kg in Agaricus bisporus.
+- Highest average Pb: 21.17 ug/kg in Pleurotus ostreatus.
+- Highest average tAs: 143.20 ug/kg in Pleurotus ostreatus.
+- Highest average Cd: 133.67 ug/kg in Lentinula edodes.
+- Largest PTWI contribution reported by the authors: Cd from Lentinula edodes, 19.12%.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+Heavy metal analysis was carried out by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry. Mercury was determined by AMA-254 direct combustion in an oxygen-rich atmosphere. Statistical analysis used SAS V9. Arsenic is reported as total As; mercury is reported as total Hg.
+
+## Implications
+
+Certification: This source contributes cultivated European mushroom occurrence values for Pb, Cd, tAs, and tHg. It is directly more comparable to cultivated mushroom products than wild-foraged studies.
+
+Courses: Useful example of species differences across common cultivated mushrooms, particularly Cd in shiitake and tAs/Pb in oyster mushrooms.
+
+App: Supports product-form distinction: cultivated mushrooms can have lower values than wild-foraged mushrooms, but species still matters.
