+
+# Woldegiorgis et al. 2015 - Lead and cadmium in edible mushrooms from Ethiopia
+
+This Ethiopian mushroom composition study measured major minerals, minor minerals, Pb, and Cd in 12 edible mushroom types. The toxic-metal table reports Pb in every mushroom sample, with dry-weight values from 1.52 to 18.0 mg/kg, and Cd detected only in Agaricus campestris at 4.08 mg/kg dry weight. The source is useful for wild and cultivated mushroom occurrence evidence, but the canned Agaricus sample should not be silently pooled with fresh or wild mushrooms.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+Table 3 reports toxic minerals in mg/kg dry weight; values are mean +/- standard error of three parallel measurements.
+
+| Mushroom type | Cd (mg/kg DW) | Pb (mg/kg DW) |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Pleurotus ostreatus | ND (<0.028) | 5.45 +/- 1.09 |
+| Lentinula edodes | ND (<0.028) | 1.52 +/- 0.73 |
+| Agaricus bisporus #1, fresh | ND (<0.028) | 18.0 +/- 0.11 |
+| Agaricus bisporus #2, canned | ND (<0.028) | 11.7 +/- 0.73 |
+| Agaricus campestris | 4.08 +/- 0.38 | 8.68 +/- 1.95 |
+| Laetiporus sulphureus | ND (<0.028) | 3.40 +/- 0.88 |
+| Termitomyces clypeatus | ND (<0.028) | 10.1 +/- 1.19 |
+| Termitomyces microcarpus #1 | ND (<0.028) | 4.03 +/- 0.30 |
+| Termitomyces aurantiacus | ND (<0.028) | 9.84 +/- 1.66 |
+| Termitomyces microcarpus #2 | ND (<0.028) | 12.3 +/- 0.32 |
+| Termitomyces letestui | ND (<0.028) | 17.2 +/- 1.51 |
+| Termitomyces sp. | ND (<0.028) | 11.9 +/- 1.72 |
+
+The abstract reports major minerals in mg/g dry basis: Na 0.41-34.8, K 3.66-42.4, Ca 0.29-6.45, Mg 0.57-2.12, P 0.71-2.82. Minor minerals in mg/kg dry basis: Fe 32.5-6835.9, Zn 26.6-87.6, Cu 5.69-45.9, Mn 0.96-138.6.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+Mushrooms were cleaned without washing, sliced, oven dried to constant weight, milled to powder, and analyzed on a dry-weight basis. The source describes mineral determination with quality-control checks against certified reference materials. Statistical reporting used mean +/- standard error from three parallel measurements and one-way ANOVA with Duncan multiple range testing.
+
+## Implications
+
+Certification: This source contributes mushroom Pb and Cd occurrence data, mostly for Ethiopian wild/cultivated mushrooms. The high dry-weight Pb values are not directly comparable to wet fresh mushrooms without moisture conversion.
+
+Courses: Useful for teaching the distinction between dry-weight mushroom values and fresh-as-consumed values; dry mushroom values can look high because water has been removed.
+
+App: Supports a wild-mushroom contamination flag for Pb; Cd was mostly non-detect except in A. campestris.
+
